lesson nature-living-or-not v=1 format=teach grade=1 unit=1 subject=nature levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Living, or not?"
  goal "Say whether a thing is living by asking whether it grows, eats and can have young, not whether it moves."

  look {
    scene 36x13 {
      row grass at=canvas(1, 1) space=2 align=end {
        art cat asset="cat"
        toadstools caps count=1 spots=5
        art kite asset="kite"
        art cup asset="cup"
      }
    }
    say """
      Ada lies in the long grass and looks. The cat is living, and so are the toadstool: they grow,
      they need food and water, and they can have young. The kite flies and the cup holds milk, but
      neither of them grows, eats or has young. Moving is not the test. A cat moves and a toadstool
      never does, and both are alive.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked nature.is-it-living k=0
    }
    practice nature.living-count count=2 seed=5 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice nature.is-it-living count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=3 hard-seed=2
    practice nature.moves-on-its-own count=2 seed=7 easy-seed=4 hard-seed=1
    practice nature.odd-one-out count=1 seed=5 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=7
    level hard {
      show nature.shows-shes-wrong k=3
    }
  }
  story {
    show nature.grass-story a=4 easy-a=3 hard-a=6
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show nature.shows-shes-wrong k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show nature.living-pairs m=3 b=2 easy-m=2 easy-b=2 hard-m=4 hard-b=3
  }
  remember {
    say "A living thing grows, eats or drinks, and can have young. Moving is not the test."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea this lesson is about is "it moves, so it is alive", and its twin, "it stays still,
    so it is not". Every question can be settled with three questions said aloud: does it grow, does
    it eat or drink, can it have young. Watch whether the child asks them, or answers from whether the
    thing moves; the kite, the boat and the rocket are there to catch the second habit, and the
    toadstool the first. The story counts creatures and a pencil, and the pencil is the point. In the
    two-star question one thing shows Ada's sentence is wrong, and the child should be able to say
    why that one and not another. The three-star question is drawn on the sheet: a line from each
    creature to each coin, and the count is the number of lines. If the child still sorts by moving
    after the core, stay here another day and go outside: find three living things and three that
    are not, and ask the three questions about each.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example (the cat) opens the core, the things asked about are a hen, a boat, a bicycle and a duck, the odd one out is a cup among animals, the kite is the thing that shows Ada is wrong, the story has three ants, and the pairs question has four lines."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the things asked about are the ones children call living because they move or shine (the kite, the rocket, the sun, the fish in a tank), a toadstool joins the counting and has to be counted as living, a second reasoning question sits in the core, the story has six ants, and the pairs question draws fifteen lines."
  }
}
